WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.876
Secret storage should match sensitivity, environment, scale, and
operational needs.

2
00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:12.914
Managed secret stores at a high level provide centralized storage, access
control, audit trails, versioning, and sometimes rotation support.

3
00:00:12.914 --> 00:00:17.328
Operating system secret stores can help protect local or workstation
secrets.

4
00:00:17.328 --> 00:00:22.863
Cloud secret managers and orchestration platforms can provide runtime
delivery to applications.

5
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:29.122
Encrypted configuration files may be appropriate in some workflows when
keys and access are handled carefully.

6
00:00:29.122 --> 00:00:31.692
Local development needs its own safe pattern.

7
00:00:31.692 --> 00:00:40.652
Developers often need credentials for tests, sandboxes, or integration
work, but those values should not live in shared plaintext files or
repository history.

8
00:00:40.652 --> 00:00:49.020
A good local pattern documents where development secrets come from, how
they are scoped, how they are excluded from commits, and how they are
replaced.

9
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.423
Convenience matters because insecure workarounds appear when the approved
path is too painful.

10
00:00:54.423 --> 00:00:56.531
Access control is the core of storage.

11
00:00:56.531 --> 00:01:00.880
A secret store is only helpful if identities and permissions are managed
well.

12
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.174
The application should retrieve only the secrets it needs.

13
00:01:04.174 --> 00:01:09.577
Developers, CI/CD systems, support tools, and runtime workloads should
have different access.

14
00:01:09.577 --> 00:01:14.584
High-impact secrets may require approval, stronger monitoring, or separate
ownership.

15
00:01:14.584 --> 00:01:18.669
Audit trails should show who or what accessed important secrets and when.

16
00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:20.250
Availability also matters.

17
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.742
If the secret store is unavailable, can the application start?

18
00:01:23.742 --> 00:01:24.994
Should it fail closed?

19
00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:26.246
Does it cache secrets?

20
00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:29.409
How does caching interact with rotation and revocation?

21
00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:30.990
These are design decisions.

22
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:40.280
The safest storage pattern is not only encrypted; it is operationally
reliable, reviewable, recoverable, and aligned with how the Python
application actually runs.
